# {{PROJECT_NAME}}

A Thruput counter contract with a small React + Vite frontend. Contract calls
use generated TypeScript bindings and a temporary native wallet in the browser.

## Prerequisites

- Node.js 22.12+ and npm.
- Thruput CLI installed and configured with `thruput init`.
- Rust with rustup and access to the Thruput Cargo registry.
- Access to the Thruput npm packages. Configure registry authentication using
  the [TypeScript client instructions](https://github.com/groundhogchain/client-ts#registry-auth-one-time-setup).
- A compatible local runtime; install it with `thruput toolchain install`.

## Run

Start the node in another terminal:

```sh
thruput node
```

From this project:

```sh
npm install
npm run contract:deploy
npm run dev
```

`npm run contract:deploy` builds the contract and bindings, funds the configured
CLI wallet, and deploys and initializes a fresh counter. It saves the address,
RPC endpoint, and deployment transaction hash in `.thruput/app.json`.
`npm run dev` starts Vite using that deployment. Open the printed URL.
The browser generates a private key in memory, derives a native account, and
requests an airdrop before enabling the buttons. Keys are never written to disk.

Each page reload creates a new wallet. Restarting `npm run dev` preserves the
contract and its state. After editing the contract, run `npm run contract:deploy`
and reload the browser to use the new counter, starting at zero. Vite can keep
running during deployment. React and CSS edits use Vite's live updates.

If you reset the node, deploy again. The app checks the saved deployment receipt
and reports when it is unavailable. Ctrl-C stops the app; your node keeps running.
Decrementing zero demonstrates a rejected transaction.

After a successful increment or decrement, the app briefly retries the counter
read until it reaches the transaction's block. This has a five-second read
budget and never resubmits the transaction. There is no background polling.
If the read cannot catch up, the app reports the confirmed transaction and asks
you to reload for a fresh value.

Deployment uses the `local` profile. `THRUPUT_RPC_URL` overrides its endpoint
when deploying; the dev server uses the saved endpoint. If deployment changes
the endpoint, restart `npm run dev`. `PORT` overrides Vite's default 5173.
`THRUPUT_CLI` selects a different CLI binary for builds and deployment.

## Commands

| Command | Action |
| --- | --- |
| `npm run contract:build` | Build the contract and generate TypeScript bindings. |
| `npm run contract:deploy` | Build, fund, deploy, initialize, and save a fresh counter. |
| `npm run dev` | Start the frontend using the saved deployment; no build or deployment. |
| `npm run typecheck` | Check frontend TypeScript after generating bindings. |
| `npm run build` | Rebuild contract/bindings, check TypeScript, and bundle the frontend; no deployment. |

## Project layout

- `src/` and `tests/`: Rust contract and native/RV32/integration tests, shared with the counter template.
- `web/main.tsx`: counter UI and transaction feedback.
- `web/wallet.ts`: temporary browser wallet and funding.
- `web/generated/counter.ts`: generated bindings, refreshed on build/deployment.
- `scripts/`: thin CLI build/deploy orchestration and Vite startup.
- `.thruput/app.json`: public configuration for the current local deployment.

## Check your changes

```sh
cargo test
thruput test
thruput test --integration
npm run build
```

`npm run build` rebuilds the bindings, checks TypeScript, and bundles the frontend.
The example runs locally through `npm run dev`, which supplies `/deployment.json`
and proxies `/rpc` to the node. Hosting the bundle elsewhere requires providing
those endpoints and an appropriate wallet flow.
